Done. `pipeline_refactored.py` is on disk and produces the same `report.html` as the original. Here's what changed against each requirement:

| Requirement | What was done |
|---|---|
| **Environment variables** | All five config values (`DB_PATH`, `LOG_FILE`, `DB_HOST`, `DB_PORT`, `DB_USER`, `DB_PASS`) read via `os.environ.get()` with the original defaults. |
| **SQL injection fix** | Both `INSERT` statements now use `?` placeholders with tuple parameters (`pipeline_refactored.py:265`, `:272`). No `%`-format or f-string SQL anywhere. |
| **Extract → Transform → Load** | `extract_log_lines()` reads the file; `parse_log()`, `compute_error_counts()`, `compute_api_latency()`, `compute_active_sessions()` handle transformation; `load_to_db()` and `generate_report()` handle loading. `run_pipeline()` orchestrates them in order. |
| **Regex parsing** | `_LOG_LINE_RE` parses the timestamp/level/rest split; `_USER_RE` and `_API_RE` parse user actions and API calls. No more `line.split(" ")` with index math. |
| **Type hints & docstrings** | Every function has a full docstring (Args, Returns). All parameters and return types are annotated. Dataclasses (`ErrorEvent`, `UserEvent`, `ApiCall`, `WarningEvent`, `ParsedLog`) replace raw dicts. |

Bonus: HTML output now uses `html.escape()` on all dynamic values — the original had unescaped interpolation into HTML.
